    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on a high-upside matchup for **James Wood** (78.42) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75). **James Wood** is our strongest play of the day, benefiting from a favorable 4.76 xERA matchup against Brandon Young and a significant park factor boost that drives his elite efficiency score. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** draws a prime assignment against Paredes, where his strong platoon advantage against the right-handed arm and favorable position in the batting order make him a high-floor start. While **Josh Naylor** (67.55) and **Jose Caballero** (62.42) round out our top-tier targets, **Jose Caballero** in particular is a key asset today; his ability to exploit a platoon edge against Bennett, combined with a 100% stolen base opportunity window against a slow-delivering starter, highlights our strategy to prioritize speed in this 5x5 Roto environment.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Zach McKinstry**, who missed the cut with a 47.14 score. While he held a marginal case, he was ultimately edged out by the superior power-projection and plate presence of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll**. We are also closely monitoring the lineup status of **Dalton Rushing**, **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, **Alex Freeland**, **Austin Riley**, **Henry Bolte**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Kyle Karros**. The risk of moving **Dalton Rushing** or **Alex Freeland** down the order could suppress their production, and we have factored these potential lineup shifts into our baseline projections. Finally, **Austin Riley** carries a warning flag; despite a wind-out advantage, his matchup against an elite arm like Logan Webb, combined with a negative BvP history and a high-tier catcher threat, keeps him in a precarious spot that demands careful monitoring as the official lineup drops.",
